  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 106 AM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThursday will be fairly similar to Wednesday under dry southwest \nflow aloft.  Temperatures will warm a couple degrees over Wed with \nhighs cresting in the upper 90s across eastern portions of the \nsoutheast plains. Any shower and thunderstorm chances stay to our \nsouth this day.  \n\nMonsoon moisture returns for Friday with the best chances across the \nsouthern mountains.  A surface trough axis across the southeast \nplains may also have sufficient convergence for some thunderstorm \ndevelopment.  Surface dew points are still relatively low (generally \nupper 30s to 40s mountains, to lower 50s eastern plains). Although\nthe heavy rainfall risk will increase again as precipitable \nwaters rebound back to 100-130% of normal, model soundings keep\nmoisture fairly high based with gusty winds the primary risk, \nespecially for northern and eastern portions of the forecast \narea. \n\nThunderstorm chances ramp up over the mountains for the weekend\nas the monsoon plume gets better established across southern \nCO. Southwest flow may pick up and entrain some tropical \nmoisture from Marie which boosts precipitable waters to 140-170%\nof normal across southern CO, with the highest values across \nthe mountains. This will put the risk for heavy rainfall and \nflash flooding over the area if we can get within the stronger \nmoisture fetch. Signals for heavier QPF appear stay to the west \nin ensemble means with Willow burn scar at a greater risk for \nflash flooding than Aspen Acres. \n\nPrecipitable waters and rain chances stay high for Monday then \nan upper trough passes by to the north and potentially disrupts \nthe deeper monsoon moisture fetch to some degree, reducing \nthunderstorm chances slightly sometime early next week. On the \nwhole, best rain chances will stay over the mountains with the \nContinental Divide seeing the greater precipitation \naccumulations. The plains will see almost daily isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms but lacking a strong signal for upslope \nflow, QPF may stay on the lighter side. There is some potential \nfor a cool front to bring some post frontal upslope flow and an \nuptick in thunderstorms across the plains on Tuesday, but this \nsignal is weaker in ensemble means, so will continue to carry \ngenerally isolated to scattered pops for the plains through the \nperiod until better confidence is gained. -KT",
